<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Capua</h1><p class="st-meta">841 CE – 1135 CE (approx.) · capital Capua</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Lombard principality of northern Campania that broke away from Benevento and later became the first Norman principality in southern Italy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the civil wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> south in the 840s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landulf-i-of-capua">Landulf I</a>, gastald of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/capua">Capua</a>, made his town practically independent of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-salerno">Salerno</a>. Arab raiders destroyed old Capua in 841, and in 856 his family founded a new Capua on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casilinum">Casilinum</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volturno">Volturno</a>. Its rulers took the title of prince by the early 10th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/capua">Capua</a> was ruled by quarrelsome branches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landulf-i-of-capua">Landulf</a>'s family, who controlled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> and much of the Liri valley. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandulf-ironhead">Pandulf Ironhead</a> (died 981) briefly united Capua, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> under imperial patronage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandulf-iv-of-capua">Pandulf IV</a>, called the Wolf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzi</a>, despoiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a> and, like his rivals, hired Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, helping the Normans gain a foothold in Campania.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-aversa">Aversa</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/capua">Capua</a> in 1058, and his family ruled it as princes for nearly eighty years. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ii-of-capua">Robert II of Capua</a> resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II</a>, the principality was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily</a> in 1135.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-capua.jpg" alt="Principality of Capua" width="320" height="460"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. MapMaster assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italy_1000_AD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>841 CE – 1135 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Capua</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pandulf Ironhead, Pandulf IV, Richard I of Capua</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>County of Capua, Capua</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=988&amp;focus=principality-of-capua" aria-label="Principality of Capua on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5029 709 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Capua lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5349 965L5349 976M5349 965L5331 963M5349 965L5362 981M5349 965L5361 966M5349 965L5333 1064M5349 965L5348 971"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5349 976h0M5331 963h0M5362 981h0M5361 966h0M5333 1064h0M5348 971h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5349 965h0"/><path 